<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan Ferrier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/2010">2010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan Ferrier was a Surinamese teacher and politician who served as prime minister from 1955 to 1958, as governor of the autonomous country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> from 1968 and as the first president of the independent republic from 1975. He was removed from office in 1980, months after the military coup of that year, and spent most of his later life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where he died aged 99.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-ferrier.jpg" alt="Johan Ferrier" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bert Verhoeff for Anefo; , Nationaal Archief, Den Haag, Rijksfotoarchief: Fotocollectie Algemeen Nederlands Fotopersbureau … · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferrier_in_Suriname_1975_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johan Henri Eliza Ferrier was born on 12 May 1910 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, and trained as a teacher. At sixteen he taught in the Saramacca District and founded his first Scout group, which still exists as the Johan Ferrier group; he co-founded the Boy Scouts van Suriname and was its first Chief Scout. In 1946, while Suriname was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colony, he was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> and sat on the National Council until 1948. He then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> to study education theory and received his doctorate in 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, by then an autonomous country within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>, he continued a career in education, serving as head of education, and was prime minister and interior minister from 1955 to 1958. He was governor from 1968 until 1975, the country's last governor-general before independence. On 25 November 1975 Suriname became independent, and Ferrier was sworn in as its first president. Dési Bouterse's coup of 1980 prompted him to resign within six months. Prime Minister Henk Chin A Sen succeeded him and on 3 September 1980 made him Grand Cordon of the Honorary Order of the Yellow Star.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ferrier then went with relatives to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and lived in Oegstgeest, where he told stories about the spider Anansi on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> children's television; a picture book, Het Grote Anansi Boek, appeared in 1986. His memoirs, Last Governor, First President, were published in 2005. His daughter Kathleen Ferrier sat in the Dutch House of Representatives. He was named the twentieth century's most important politician in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> in 1999. He died of heart failure at home in Oegstgeest on 4 January 2010, aged 99, and was buried there on 11 January. Queen Beatrix paid tribute, and the Johan Ferrier Fund, supporting Surinamese educational and cultural projects, was launched on 12 May 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johan Ferrier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Autonomy under the Charter of 1954 was followed by rapid moves towards independence under Prime Minister Henck Arron, and Suriname became an independent republic on 25 November 1975, with <b>Johan Ferrier</b> as its first president and a large Dutch aid settlement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>. Died in Oegstgeest, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=5.87,-55.17&amp;year=1975" aria-label="Paramaribo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johan Ferrier"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5411 4499h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5411 4499h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=5.87,-55.17&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
